# Simulation pipeline

Companion to `X_ray_phase_contrast_retrieval.py`. That script processes
real measured X-ray images from DanMAX. This one
(`X_ray_phase_contrast_simulation.py`) does the opposite: it builds a
known, synthetic sample from scratch, simulates what the detector should
see, and then runs the same kind of phase reconstruction on that simulated
data. Because the "real" answer is known here, this script can check how
accurate each reconstruction method actually is — something you can't do
with real measurements, where you never know the true phase to begin with.

## What's in `X_ray_phase_contrast_simulation.py`

1. **Builds a virtual sample.** A Siemens star test pattern (the same
   spoked-wheel design as the real target), made of a thin tantalum
   absorber pattern on a silicon-carbide membrane. The optical properties
   (how much the material absorbs and phase-shifts X-rays) are looked up
   from physical tables, not guessed.

2. **Simulates what the detector would see.** Using the exact same
   beamline geometry as the real experiment (same distances, same
   magnifications), it propagates the X-ray wave from the sample to the
   detector using wave optics (Fresnel diffraction) — not a shortcut
   approximation. It also adds the things that blur or add noise to a real
   measurement: the finite size of the X-ray source, the detector's own
   blur, and photon-counting noise. You end up with five synthetic
   "detector images," just like the five real distances in the actual
   experiment.

3. **Reconstructs the phase**, using the same family of algorithms as the
   real-data script (CTF, homoCTF, Paganin, sglDstCTF), tried with
   different combinations of the five distances.

4. **Checks the answer.** Since the true phase of the virtual sample is
   known exactly, the script can directly compare each reconstruction
   against the ground truth — not just estimate resolution the way the
   real-data script has to.

5. **Measures resolution and contrast**, the same way as the real-data
   script (MTF, Fourier-domain resolution, contrast-to-noise), so the two
   scripts' numbers are directly comparable.

6. **Compares against a real measurement.** The last part of the script
   loads an actual reconstructed image (produced earlier by the real-data
   pipeline), lines it up pixel-for-pixel with the matching simulation,
   and reports how closely they agree — this is the key sanity check for
   whether the whole simulation is realistic, not just internally
   consistent.

## Before running it

Near the top of the script there's a CONFIG section — set these to point
at your own files.

If you don't have the NIST optical-constants pipeline, the script falls
back to `xraylib` automatically — just make sure it's installed.

You'll also need: `numpy`, `scipy`, `matplotlib`, `pandas`, `h5py`. It also
relies on the same helper modules as the real-data script
(`usefull_functions`, `PBI_phase_retrieval_functions`).

## A couple of things worth knowing

- This script assembles what used to be several separate notebook cells,
  some of which had been duplicated with slightly different settings
  along the way. During cleanup, the duplicates were merged and a few
  small inconsistencies between them (like the same setting being
  redefined with different values in different places) were resolved and
  clearly labeled in the code with `NOTE` comments — worth a read if a
  number looks different from what you remember getting before.
- The last section (comparing against a real measurement) needs a `.npy`
  file that was already produced by the real-data pipeline
  (`X_ray_phase_contrast_retrieval.py`) — run that first if you don't have
  one yet.
- Running the whole script end-to-end (all five distances, every
  reconstruction method, the full resolution/CNR analysis, and the
  real-vs-simulated comparison) takes a while — it's meant to be run in
  one sitting, not re-run from scratch for small tweaks.
